<h2>How the matching works</h2>

<p>For every pair of consecutive frames of a code group:</p>

<ol>
  <li>both are parsed into syntax trees, flattened to generic <c>{ label, text, children }</c> nodes</li>
  <li><b>top-down</b>: identical subtrees are matched, tallest first</li>
  <li><b>bottom-up</b>: containers are matched by how many matched descendants they share</li>
  <li><b>extra cues</b>: text of identifiers and literals, position in the file, the node's slot in its parent</li>
  <li>a <b>leaf pass</b> rescues unique identifiers inside matched containers</li>
</ol>

<p>
  Matched tokens become the same DOM element, so a CSS transition moves them; unmatched old tokens fade
  out, unmatched new ones fade in.
</p>

<notes>
  Based on GumTree (Falleri et al., 2014). Details in README and src/gumtree.ts.
</notes>
